fact WorkItem(id: "WI-731", description: some(value: "WI-714 follow-up: join -- support COLUMN-NAME COLLISION and 1-collapsed/membership operands (the merged-schema cases the first increment rejects). CONTEXT: proposal 052 join increment (6185dbc2). join(r1, r2, cond) wraps guarded(conjunction(r1.query, r2.query), goal); the merged schema T = Concat[A = r1.T, B = r2.T], a bare type CONSTRUCTOR reduced by an internal typer op (reduce_concat_in_type / concat_named_tuple_types) at the return-type normalization boundary. FIRST increment scope: a single atomic predicate over two MULTI-column relations with DISJOINT column names. DEFERRED (today a LOUD error, correctly -- never a silent wrong schema): (a) a SHARED column name across the two rows ('eq(c.x, q.x)' when both have x) -- the merged schema would have to QUALIFY or RENAME the clashing column, and join_run's hole fill (by name, disjoint in this increment) would need the same disambiguation; (b) a 1-COLLAPSED or membership operand -- a 1-collapse DROPPED the column name at the schema boundary, so Concat has no name to merge and it would have to be RECOVERED. FIX DIRECTION: decide the collision surface first (qualify by operand -- 'c.x'/'q.x' -> left.x/right.x -- vs require an explicit rename via project before the join); then teach Concat's reducer + join_run's fill the same rule, sharing ONE name-resolution contract so typer and eval cannot drift (the rule_head_var_slots precedent). For (b), the 1-collapse is done at the typer's schema boundary (OQ5 keeps the stored element as the 1-field record), so the name may be recoverable there rather than reconstructed. ACCEPTANCE: a self-join r.join(r, ...) and a shared-column-name join produce a correct, unambiguous merged schema and run end-to-end; a 1-collapsed operand either works or stays a loud error with a message naming the fix; nothing keyed on join's identity in the typer (Concat stays a general type constructor); existing wi714_join tests green; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-714"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Open)

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-731", author: "claude", content: "SCOPE DECIDED (user, 2026-08-18) — the collision surface is EXPLICIT RENAMING, made cheap by a new rename operator; automatic qualification is REJECTED. The 1-collapsed/membership half is SPLIT OUT of this ticket.

WHERE THE NAMES COME FROM (read, not assumed): rule_head_var_slots (typing.rs:7120) is the sole source — a POSITIONAL head arg \'?name\' takes the LOGICAL VARIABLE\'s own name, a NAMED head arg \'who: ?v\' takes the LABEL. So a column name is chosen by whoever wrote the RULE, and a collision is two independently-authored rules picking the same variable name (?name, ?id, ?x are exactly the ones people reuse). A self-join r.join(r, ...) collides on EVERY column by construction.

WHERE IT IS REFUSED TODAY (measured by probe on the current tree): at LOAD, in concat_named_tuple_types (typing.rs:3005) — \'1:1: type mismatch in join_run.return (op-return): expected a well-formed type projection, got [concat] operands share the field name [name] — a merged schema requires disjoint field names (rename one, or project first)\'. join_run carries a runtime backstop for a relation built past the typer via reflect.

IT IS TWO PROBLEMS, AND ONLY ONE IS THE SCHEMA — this is not in the description and it changes what the guard is FOR. (1) SCHEMA: Concat would build a named tuple with two [name] fields, which label-distinctness forbids. (2) THE HOLE: binder_field_access names a column hole by the FIELD SYMBOL ALONE, discarding WHICH BINDER it came from, so \'eq(c.name, q.name)\' compiles to two holes both named [name] and fill_recipe_holes maps both to the SAME column variable — the condition becomes \'eq(?x, ?x)\', VACUOUSLY TRUE, and every pair of rows joins. The function\'s own doc states the dependency (\'the field name alone identifies the column\'), true only while the names are disjoint. So today\'s guard is not merely protecting a schema; it is the only thing between the user and a silently unfiltered cartesian product.

CONSEQUENCE FOR THIS TICKET: under the EXPLICIT surface the hole ambiguity is UNREACHABLE BY CONSTRUCTION — Concat refuses a shared name at LOAD, so no program in which both holes could exist ever loads, and join_run\'s merged-name guard fires before fill_recipe_holes on the reflect path. Do NOT \'fix\' the hole here: the change would have no drivable control (a test would pass with and without it), which is the WI-1078 shape. State the invariant and its two enforcement sites at binder_field_access instead. It is the AUTOMATIC-QUALIFICATION design that would have made the hole live — that is the strongest reason the two halves had to be decided together.

WHY NOT AUTOMATIC QUALIFICATION (left.x / right.x): it destroys SCHEMA LOCALITY. Qualify only on collision and a rule author ADDING A COLUMN silently renames columns in someone else\'s downstream join result — the result schema stops being a function of the two relations and becomes a function of their accidental name overlap. Qualify always and every join result forces qualified reads while every disjoint join that works today changes shape. Both spellings also invent names no rule declared, which the reader must then spell, and they NEST under a chained join.

THE DELIVERABLE: a RENAME-ONLY operator. [project] already renames (r.(person: name, years: age), wi732) but it SELECTS TOO — to rename one column of a six-column relation you list all six, which is the actual ergonomic gap and the only real objection to the explicit surface. Add a Rename[T = p.T, Map = R] type constructor beside Concat / Without / Project, reducing at the SAME return-type normalization boundary. Rename IS Project over \'Map plus identity on the rest\', so reuse Project\'s map ENCODING unchanged (result-key -> denoted source-name, the SAME DIRECTION as r.(a: f1) — new: old); the runtime is project_run\'s column rebuild with the unmentioned columns kept.

ORDER IS IDENTITY: a renamed column must keep its ORIGINAL POSITION — rename replaces a name IN PLACE and must not hoist renamed columns to the front. A tuple\'s component order is part of its identity, so a reordering rename would silently change the schema type of every downstream consumer. Assert the order in a test with at least three columns and a MIDDLE one renamed; a two-column fixture cannot distinguish \'in place\' from \'renamed first\'.

OPEN, THE ONLY DESIGN QUESTION LEFT — THE SURFACE for naming the source column. [project] gets its source names from the distribute-dot \'r.(a: f1)\', where f1 parses as a field access on the receiver and a typer recognizer reads the name-keyed row tuple. Rename needs that same source-name channel plus \'keep the rest\'. Two candidates: (i) a variadic-capture operation \'rename[R](p: Relation, ...args: R)\' in fix\'s shape, which needs a way to write a bare COLUMN NAME as an argument — fix\'s captured entries are VALUES, not names, so this is NOT the same channel and must not be assumed to be; (ii) an ellipsis on the EXISTING projection surface, \'r.(who: name, ...)\', which makes rename a variant of projection rather than a second construct and reads exactly as \'Rename = Project that keeps the rest\'. (ii) is the smaller language and reuses the recognizer that already resolves source names; it costs a grammar change.

ALSO IN SCOPE, inline (small, and this ticket touches the path): the collision diagnostic points at \'1:1\' and blames \'join_run.return\', an operation the author never wrote — the WI-757 class already fixed for guarded_of\'s rejections. Locate it at the join call site and name the rename operator as the fix.

ACCEPTANCE, revised: a self-join and a shared-column-name join each RUN end-to-end after an explicit rename, selecting the right rows (drive the values, not \'it loads\'); a rename preserves column ORDER, pinned by a middle-column rename over three columns; an un-renamed collision stays a LOUD LOAD error, now located at the join and naming the rename operator; nothing keyed on join\'s or rename\'s identity in the typer (Rename stays a general type constructor, as Concat/Without/Project are); the hole/binder invariant is DOCUMENTED at binder_field_access with both enforcement sites, not changed; existing wi714_join and wi732 tests green; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh.

NOTE ON THIS ENTRY: its first version was written through a double-quoted shell string and lost every backtick-quoted identifier to command substitution — concat, name, project, rename and the Rename[...] type all vanished mid-sentence, leaving grammatical but meaningless prose. Rewritten in place. Backticks are avoided here for that reason; square brackets stand in where a code span was meant.", at: "2026-08-18T07:52:46Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-731", author: "claude", content: "SPLIT RECORDED: the 1-collapsed / membership operand half of this ticket is now WI-1128. This ticket keeps ONLY the column-name collision, whose surface is the explicit rename operator decided above. The two share no mechanism — collision is about a name that exists TWICE, 1-collapse about a name that exists ZERO times — and the acceptance above has been rewritten to the collision half alone.", at: "2026-08-18T07:54:59Z")
